## The domain for containers the operator has decided may hold the Docker
## socket (SPEC.md §spec:container-updates).
##
## The machine stays Enforcing and container_t - every container on the machine -
## still has no route to the Docker socket. This module does not change that. It
## adds a SECOND domain, which nothing runs in unless something names it:
##
##     docker run --security-opt label=type:kantainer_socket_client_t ...
##
## kantainer-watchtower.service names it. An operator deploying a stack from
## Portainer can name it too, which is the supported way to run anything else
## that needs the socket - a reverse proxy watching for container labels, a log
## viewer - without handing the socket to every container on the machine.
##
## WHY THIS IS NOT A SECOND kantainer_portainer_t. Portainer's domain also owns
## kantainer_portainer_file_t: its database, the administrator hash and its TLS
## key. Putting Watchtower there would give it that too. This domain owns no file
## type anyone labels anything with, so it carries the socket exemption and
## nothing else.
##
## WHAT THIS DOMAIN IS AND IS NOT WORTH. A container holding the Docker socket is
## root-equivalent: it can start another container with any label it likes and
## mount anything. The separation here is NOT a wall around a socket holder, and
## reading it as one would be a mistake. It is the difference between one named
## opt-in and every container the operator ever deploys through Portainer, which
## is the same argument §spec:portainer-service made for Portainer's own domain.
##
## Deciding a container may hold the socket is therefore the same decision as
## giving it root on the machine. docs/watchtower.md says so to the operator.

policy_module(kantainer_socket_client, 1.0.0)

gen_require(`
	attribute container_domain;
	attribute container_net_domain;
	attribute container_file_type;
	type container_runtime_t;
	type container_file_t;
')

# The template needs a file type named after this domain's prefix. Nothing on the
# machine is labelled with it and no .fc file places it: this domain keeps no
# state of its own. It exists so the template below has its second argument.
type kantainer_socket_client_file_t;
container_file(kantainer_socket_client_file_t)

container_domain_template(kantainer_socket_client, kantainer_socket_client)

# Watchtower reaches registries itself to compare digests before asking the
# daemon to pull, so this domain talks to the network. container_domain alone
# cannot open a socket - container-selinux keeps network access on a separate
# attribute - and without this the container starts and then fails every check
# with a DNS error that looks like a broken network rather than a policy denial.
typeattribute kantainer_socket_client_t container_net_domain;

# THE ACCESS EVERY CONTAINER HAS, WHICH THIS DOMAIN DOES NOT INHERIT.
#
# Identical in purpose and in consequence to the block in
# kantainer_portainer.te, which explains it at length. The short version, because
# the `map` line is the one that costs a day: container-selinux grants
# container_domain `entrypoint` on container_file_t, so execve succeeds and the
# loader's mmap of the binary is then denied. The process dies of SIGSEGV before
# it can log a word - systemd reports status=139, the journal carries nothing
# from the container at all, and the only evidence on the machine is the AVC.
#
# This widens nothing: MCS gives each container its own categories, so what is
# granted here is what any container already has over its own layers.
manage_dirs_pattern(kantainer_socket_client_t, container_file_t, container_file_t)
manage_files_pattern(kantainer_socket_client_t, container_file_t, container_file_t)
manage_lnk_files_pattern(kantainer_socket_client_t, container_file_t, container_file_t)
exec_files_pattern(kantainer_socket_client_t, container_file_t, container_file_t)
allow kantainer_socket_client_t container_file_t:dir_file_class_set map;

# Docker starts containers with NoNewPrivileges, which needs this transition.
container_runtime_typebounds(kantainer_socket_client_t)

# The exemption itself: writing the socket file, and connecting to the daemon
# behind it. Both come from container_stream_connect(), which container-selinux
# maintains for exactly this purpose - the rules are not written out here, so
# they stay correct as upstream changes them.
container_stream_connect(kantainer_socket_client_t)
